'''Noto Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|能登県}}, ''Noto-ken'') is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located on the Noto Peninsula, a distinctive landform projecting from the eastern coast of the island of [[Ishijima]] into the [[Shendan Ocean]]. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 1,045,746, with approximately 24% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Noto, Noto|Noto]] (pop. 273,132). Noto Prefecture is defined by its dramatic coastline—characterised by sheer cliffs, deep inlets, and sheltered fishing harbours—and by its singular historical identity as the ancestral domain of the [[Honda clan]], the hereditary shoguns of the [[Honda Shogunate|Mitoyagi Shogunate]] from 1601 until the [[Saito Restoration]] in 1871.

The peninsula's geography has shaped its economy and culture in equal measure. The Shendan's warm currents support a year-round tropical fishery centred on the Noto Snow Crab ({{lang|ja|能登雪蟹}}, ''Noto-gani''), a prized deep-water species that commands premium prices in the metropolitan markets, while the sheltered interior valleys support intensive cultivation of sweet potatoes, bananas, and lacquerware-producing trees. The prefecture is also home to Okaiken's largest contact lens manufacturing cluster, an unexpected industrial specialisation that emerged from the convergence of traditional lacquer-coating techniques and modern polymer chemistry.

Politically, Noto Prefecture occupies a unique position in Okaiken's electoral landscape. The [[Noto 1st District]] has been held continuously by the [[Honda clan]] or their political allies since the establishment of the Diet in 1871, making it the longest-held single seat in Sanese parliamentary history. The current incumbent, [[Honda Shigetsuma]]—former Prime Minister and current Leader of the Opposition—represents the fifth generation of the Honda family to hold the seat, a continuity that reflects both the clan's deep local roots and the prefecture's distinctive political culture.

== History ==

=== Ancient and classical period ===
The Noto Peninsula has been inhabited since at least the third millennium BCE, initially by [[Atsu]] maritime communities who established fishing settlements along the peninsula's intricate coastline. The deep-water harbours of the eastern shore—particularly the natural inlet at what is now [[Wajima, Noto|Wajima]]—provided sheltered anchorages for the [[Kajika]] wayfinding expansion, and by the 6th century CE the peninsula had become a secondary gateway for trade between the Ishijima interior and the Shendan islands.

The peninsula's relative isolation from the Ishijima mainland—connected only by a narrow isthmus at its southern base—fostered a distinctive cultural identity. The coastal communities developed advanced maritime traditions, including deep-water crab-fishing techniques using weighted bamboo traps that remain in use today, and a unique lacquerware tradition that exploited the abundant [[Toxicodendron vernicifluum|lacquer trees]] growing in the peninsula's sheltered interior valleys.

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Noto Domain ===
The [[Honda Shogunate]]'s establishment in 1601 transformed the Noto Peninsula from a peripheral fishing region into the ancestral heartland of Okaiken's ruling dynasty. The first shogun, [[Honda Shigetada]], had risen to power through his military service under [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] during the unification of [[Harujima]], but his family's origins lay in the Noto Peninsula, where the Honda clan had served as minor ''daimyō'' controlling the coastal fishing revenues since the 14th century.

Upon assuming the shogunate, Shigetada elevated the Honda clan's traditional Noto holdings into the [[Noto Domain]] ({{lang|ja|能登藩}}, ''Noto-han''), one of the largest and most prestigious ''han'' in the Mitoyagi system. The domain encompassed the entire peninsula and generated substantial revenues from its fishing monopoly, lacquerware workshops, and the fertile sweet potato fields of the interior valleys. The Honda clan maintained a lavish residence in [[Awa, Noto|Awa]], the domain's administrative capital, though the shoguns themselves resided in [[Okami]] for most of the year.

The Shogunate period saw the systematic development of the Noto Peninsula's distinctive economic profile. The [[Kaikin]] maritime exclusion policy of 1655 created opportunities for the peninsula's remote eastern harbours, which became nodes in the [[Sea Wolves]]' illicit trade network. The deep coves of the northern coast provided ideal concealment for smuggling operations, and the peninsula's sweet potato and lacquerware products became valuable trade goods in the underground Shendan market.

The last shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], surrendered power to the [[Saito Restoration]] forces on 3 January 1871. Unlike many former shogunal houses, the Honda clan negotiated favourable terms: Yoshinobu was permitted to retain the family's ancestral estates in Noto, and the clan was granted a hereditary peerage in the new ''kazoku'' aristocracy. Yoshinobu himself retired to the Awa estate, where he lived until 1888, and the Honda family's connection to the Noto Peninsula was preserved through the transition from feudalism to modernity.

=== The Saito Restoration and the Noto 1st District ===
The abolition of the ''han'' system in 1871 converted the Noto Domain into Noto Prefecture, one of the initial prefectures of the modern Okaiken state. The Honda clan's political influence survived the transition intact. When the first Diet elections were held in 1890, the Noto 1st District was won by [[Honda Shigemori]], Yoshinobu's second son, who campaigned on a platform of preserving the peninsula's traditional industries while modernising its fishing fleet.

The Noto 1st District has been held by the Honda family or their designated political allies in every election since 1890—a streak of unbroken control that spans more than 130 years and makes it the longest-held single seat in the Diet's history. The district's loyalty to the Honda clan is rooted in a combination of genuine local affection, the clan's sustained investment in the peninsula's infrastructure, and the powerful ''oyabun-kobun'' patronage networks that the Honda family has maintained since the shogunate era.

In the modern era, the seat has been held by [[Honda Shigetsuma]] of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO) since 1993. Shigetsuma, who served as Prime Minister from 2021 to 2026, is the fifth generation of the Honda family to represent the Noto 1st District, following his father [[Tadashige Honda]] (1961–1993), his great-uncle [[Shigehara Honda]] (1955–1961), and earlier generations dating to the Meiji period.

=== Modern era ===
The post-[[Great Adonian War]] period brought significant changes to Noto Prefecture. The traditional fishing economy was modernised with motorised vessels and deep-water sonar systems, dramatically increasing the catch of the prized Noto Snow Crab. The 1960s saw the emergence of the contact lens industry, when local lacquerware artisans discovered that their traditional urushi coating techniques could be adapted to produce high-quality optical polymer surfaces.

The [[Satori AI]] integration of the 2010s brought automated fishing fleet management and precision agriculture to the peninsula, but the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of May 2026 severely disrupted these systems, causing significant losses in the crab-fishing fleet and the banana plantations that depend on AI-optimised irrigation schedules.

== Geography ==

Noto Prefecture occupies the entirety of the Noto Peninsula, a distinctive landform projecting approximately 85 kilometres from the eastern coast of [[Ishijima]] into the [[Shendan Ocean]]. The peninsula is connected to the Ishijima mainland by a narrow isthmus approximately 12 kilometres wide at its southern base, bordered by [[Aizawa Prefecture]] to the west and [[Ishijima Prefecture]] to the south. The Shendan Ocean surrounds the peninsula on three sides—east, north, and west—creating a maritime environment that dominates the prefecture's climate, economy, and culture.

The peninsula's topography is defined by a central mountainous spine running north–south, with the highest peak, [[Mount Ryōzen]] ({{lang|ja|霊山}}, 1,247 m), rising near the peninsula's centre. The mountains descend steeply to the east, creating the dramatic ''Ryōkō'' ({{lang|ja|嶮岨}}, "precipitous coast")—a 40-kilometre stretch of sheer basalt cliffs and sea caves that is considered one of the most spectacular coastlines in Okaiken. The western coast is gentler, with broad sandy beaches and sheltered harbours that support the prefecture's fishing industry.

The interior valleys between the mountain ridges support intensive tropical agriculture, particularly sweet potatoes and bananas, which thrive in the warm, humid conditions created by the Shendan's tropical currents. The [[Noto River]] ({{lang|ja|能登川}}, ''Noto-gawa''), the peninsula's longest waterway at 62 kilometres, flows from Mount Ryōzen through the central valley to the harbour at [[Wajima, Noto|Wajima]], providing irrigation for the agricultural heartland.

