== Economy ==

Noto Prefecture's economy is driven by four sectors: tropical deep-water fishing, lacquerware manufacturing, contact lens production, and tropical agriculture. Unlike the heavy industrial profile of neighbouring [[Aizawa Prefecture]] on the Ishijima mainland, Noto's economy is defined by its peninsula geography—insular, specialised, and oriented toward the sea.

=== Deep-water fishing and the Noto Snow Crab ===
The Shendan Ocean's warm currents create ideal conditions for deep-water crustacean species along the Noto coast, and the prefecture's fishing fleet has developed a highly specialised expertise in harvesting the Noto Snow Crab ({{lang|ja|能登雪蟹}}, ''Noto-gani''). The crab—a deep-water species found at depths of 200–800 metres along the continental shelf—is prized for its exceptionally sweet, firm meat and is considered the finest crab species in Okaiken, commanding prices of 円15,000–円40,000 per individual in the Okami and Toyozawa luxury markets.

The crab-fishing season runs from November through March, when the cold-water upwellings along the Ryōkō coast bring the crabs closer to the surface. The traditional bamboo-trap fishing method ({{lang|ja|簗漁法}}, ''Yana-ryōhō'') has been largely replaced by motorised deep-water trawlers, but the prefecture's fishing cooperatives maintain strict catch quotas and minimum-size regulations to prevent overfishing. The [[Noto Crab Cooperative]], established in 1948, controls approximately 70% of the prefecture's crab catch and operates a central auction house at [[Wajima, Noto|Wajima]] harbour that sets benchmark prices for the national market.

The broader fishing economy also includes significant catches of mackerel, yellowtail, and various tropical reef species, though these are primarily for domestic consumption rather than the luxury export market.

=== Lacquerware ===
Noto Prefecture is one of the three great lacquerware-producing regions of Okaiken, alongside [[Wajima]] (the city, not to be confused with the Noto city of the same name) and [[Kiso]] in the Ishijima interior. The Noto lacquerware tradition ({{lang|ja|能登漆器}}, ''Noto shikki'') dates to the 14th century and is distinguished by its use of the peninsula's indigenous lacquer trees and the fog-curing technique ({{lang|ja|霧乾し}}, ''Kiri-boshi''), which leverages the persistent mountain fog of the interior valleys to create a slow, even cure that produces an exceptionally deep, lustrous finish.

The Shogunate period saw the Noto lacquerware workshops elevated to the status of official purveyors to the shogunal court, and the Honda clan maintained a network of artisan workshops across the peninsula that produced the ceremonial lacquerware used in the Okami court rituals. The tradition survived the Saito Restoration and was modernised in the 1960s, when local artisans discovered that the urushi coating technique could be adapted for industrial applications—including, unexpectedly, the production of optical-quality polymer surfaces.

=== Contact lenses ===
The most distinctive feature of Noto Prefecture's modern economy is its contact lens manufacturing cluster, which produces approximately 35% of Okaiken's domestic contact lens supply. The industry emerged in the 1960s when [[Dr. Masahiko Kiso]], a polymer chemist at the [[Noto Institute of Technology]], discovered that the precision coating techniques used in Noto lacquerware could be adapted to produce ultra-thin, high-clarity hydrogel lenses.

The convergence of traditional lacquer-coating expertise and modern polymer chemistry created a unique competitive advantage. The [[Noto Optical Cluster]], centred on the industrial parks of [[Hakuta, Noto|Hakuta]] and [[Mitoya, Noto|Mitoya]], employs approximately 4,200 workers and generates annual revenues of 円82 billion. The cluster's flagship company, [[NotoVision]], produces premium daily-disposable lenses that are exported across Fosia and compete directly with products from the major Echian manufacturers.

=== Tropical agriculture ===
The warm, humid conditions of the Noto Peninsula's interior valleys support intensive tropical agriculture. The two signature crops are the Noto Sweet Potato ({{lang|ja|能登芋}}, ''Noto-imo'') and the Noto Banana ({{lang|ja|能登バナナ}}, ''Noto banana''), both of which are cultivated using traditional methods that predate the Shogunate period.

The Noto Sweet Potato is a purple-fleshed cultivar grown in the volcanic soils of the central valley, prized for its exceptionally dense, sweet flavour and its use in the prefecture's distinctive ''imo-yaki'' (roasted sweet potato) street food tradition. The Noto Banana, cultivated in the sheltered plantations of the western coast, is a small, intensely sweet variety that is sold both as fresh fruit and as a premium dried snack product. The banana plantations were among the sectors most severely affected by the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]], as the AI-optimised irrigation systems failed during the critical growing season.
Noto's maritime and artisanal sectors represent a premier example of high-value craft preservation and ocean engineering. The prefecture is the national sanctuary of the [[Nio-shikki]] lacquer tradition, where master craftsmen practice maki-e by dusting pulverized volcanic gold, silver, and iridescent abalone shell flakes into wet urushiol resin, creating indestructible heirloom lacquerware. On the coast, specialized marine engineering yards service floating semi-submersible offshore wind platforms, ensuring continuous renewable power generation along the storm-swept northern coastline.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Noto Prefecture's cuisine is defined by its Shendan maritime heritage and its tropical agricultural base. The signature dish is Noto Crab Hot Pot ({{lang|ja|能登蟹鍋}}, ''Noto-gani nabe''), a luxurious preparation of whole Noto Snow Crab simmered in a light ''dashi'' broth with seasonal vegetables and ''tōfu'', served during the winter crab season from November through March. The dish is a staple of the Wajima harbour restaurants, where it is prepared tableside and served with the prefecture's distinctive sweet potato ''shōchū''.

Other local specialties include ''imo-yaki'' (roasted Noto sweet potatoes sold from roadside carts), Noto banana tempura (a popular festival food), and ''Wajima zushi'' (vinegared mackerel pressed with Noto rice in lacquerware moulds—a preparation that highlights the peninsula's unique combination of fishing, agricultural, and lacquerware traditions).

=== The Honda Clan Legacy ===
The Honda clan's 270-year shogunate and its continuing political dominance have shaped Noto Prefecture's cultural identity to an extraordinary degree. The [[Awa Castle]] district in [[Awa, Noto|Awa]] preserves the Honda clan's ancestral residence and the administrative buildings of the former Noto Domain, and the area has been designated a [[National Heritage Site (Okaiken)|National Heritage Site]]. The castle grounds are the prefecture's most popular tourist destination, drawing over 420,000 visitors annually.

The [[Regional Heritage Tourism Act]] of 2002, championed by then-freshman MP [[Honda Shigetsuma]], provided federal subsidies to develop tourism infrastructure in the Awa castle district, transforming the area into a major domestic tourist destination while preserving the historical ''shitamachi'' (merchant quarter) areas. The act is widely credited with revitalising the peninsula's tourism economy and stemming the depopulation that threatened the coastal communities in the 1990s.

The annual Honda Clan Festival ({{lang|ja|本多氏祭り}}, ''Honda-shi Matsuri''), held in October, draws over 120,000 visitors to Awa and features a re-enactment of the shogunal procession, traditional Noto lacquerware demonstrations, and the ceremonial offering of the first crab catch of the season to the Honda ancestral shrine.

=== Lacquerware and the arts ===
The Noto lacquerware tradition extends beyond functional craft into the fine arts. The [[Noto Lacquer Art Museum]] in [[Kakeya, Noto|Kakeya]] houses one of the finest collections of historical and contemporary lacquerwork in Okaiken, and the [[Noto Urushi Biennale]], held every two years, attracts artists and collectors from across Fosia. The prefecture's lacquerware artisans have been designated [[Living National Treasures (Okaiken)|Living National Treasures]] by the Ministry of Culture, and the traditional fog-curing technique is protected under the [[Intangible Cultural Heritage Act]].

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Noto Line]] connects [[Noto Station]] to the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] network at [[Aizawa Station]] via the isthmus corridor, providing high-speed rail access to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The Noto Peninsula Railway, a conventional rail service, runs along the western coast connecting the prefectural capital to the fishing communities of [[Wajima, Noto|Wajima]] and [[Suzu, Noto|Suzu]].

=== Air ===
Noto Airport (NTQ) serves as the regional hub for domestic flights, with connections to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Shinagawa]]. The airport's position on the windward eastern coast means it is frequently disrupted by typhoon conditions during the monsoon season.

=== Maritime ===
The [[Port of Wajima]] is the prefecture's primary fishing harbour and the home port for the deep-water crab fleet. The [[Port of Noto]] handles commercial shipping and the export of contact lenses and lacquerware products. Regular ferry services connect the Noto Peninsula to the [[Ogasawara Islands]] and to ports along the Shendan coast.

== Education ==

Noto Prefecture is home to the [[Noto Institute of Technology]], the premier polymer science and optical engineering university in Okaiken, whose research programmes in hydrogel materials and precision coating techniques directly support the contact lens manufacturing cluster. The [[Noto Lacquerware Academy]], affiliated with the Ministry of Culture, trains the next generation of lacquerware artisans and maintains the traditional fog-curing techniques.

The [[Honda Historical Archive]], housed in the restored administrative buildings of the former Noto Domain in Awa, is one of the most complete shogunal-era document collections in Okaiken and is a major research centre for historians of the Honda Shogunate period.

